    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. could u expand a bit more on the liquidity part? specifically how the intraday pricing works for etfs vs the end-of-day pricing for mutual funds. i want to make sure i explain to the client why they might see price changes while they're trying to trade an etf but not with a mutual fund.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "With ETFs, intraday pricing reflects real-time market forces; the price you see can change even within seconds as buyers and sellers determine the current market value. Because of this continuous trading, you can place limit or market orders at prices that are actively changing throughout the trading session. Conversely, mutual funds calculate a single Net Asset Value (NAV) after the market closes, meaning any purchase or sale reflects that single, final computed price for the day.",
      "source": "target",
